Lab 14: Hyperconverged Infrastructure in OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Deploy and manage hyperconverged infrastructure (HCI) in OpenStack by combining compute and storage resources on the same nodes.
Monitor performance and optimize resource allocation in an HCI environment.
Troubleshoot common issues in hyperconverged OpenStack deployments.
Prerequisites
A working OpenStack environment (e.g., deployed via OpenStack-Ansible, Kolla-Ansible, or MicroStack).
SSH access to OpenStack controller and compute nodes.
Basic familiarity with OpenStack CLI (openstack, nova, cinder commands).
Admin privileges for OpenStack services.
Software Requirements:
OpenStack (Queens or later)
Ceph (for distributed storage)
Prometheus + Grafana (for monitoring)
Lab Setup
Environment Verification
Ensure all nodes (controller and compute) are reachable and OpenStack services are running:

openstack compute service list
openstack volume service list
Install Ceph on Compute Nodes
Hyperconverged infrastructure requires shared storage. We'll use Ceph for distributed block storage.

On each compute node, install Ceph packages:

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y ceph-mon ceph-osd ceph-mgr ceph-common
Task 1: Configure Compute and Storage on the Same Nodes
Subtask 1.1: Configure Ceph for Hyperconverged Storage
Initialize Ceph Cluster
On the first compute node, generate a Ceph configuration:

sudo ceph-deploy new <compute-node-1>
Deploy Ceph Monitors and OSDs
Deploy Ceph monitors and object storage daemons (OSDs) on all compute nodes:

sudo ceph-deploy mon create-initial
sudo ceph-deploy osd create --data /dev/sdb <compute-node-1> <compute-node-2>
Verify Ceph Health
Ensure Ceph cluster is healthy:

sudo ceph -s
Expected Output: HEALTH_OK

Subtask 1.2: Integrate Ceph with OpenStack
Configure Cinder for Ceph Backend
Edit /etc/cinder/cinder.conf on the controller:

[DEFAULT]
enabled_backends = ceph

[ceph]
volume_driver = cinder.volume.drivers.rbd.RBDDriver
rbd_pool = volumes
rbd_ceph_conf = /etc/ceph/ceph.conf
rbd_flatten_volume_from_snapshot = false
rbd_max_clone_depth = 5
rbd_store_chunk_size = 4
rados_connect_timeout = -1
Restart Cinder Services

sudo systemctl restart cinder-volume
Verify Integration
Create a test volume:

openstack volume create --size 1 test_volume
openstack volume list
Expected Outcome: Volume status should be available.

Task 2: Monitor Performance and Optimize Resources
Subtask 2.1: Deploy Monitoring Stack
Install Prometheus and Grafana
On the controller node:

sudo apt-get install -y prometheus grafana
Configure Prometheus to Scrape Ceph and Nova Metrics
Edit /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml:

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'ceph'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['<compute-node-1>:9283', '<compute-node-2>:9283']
  - job_name: 'nova'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['<controller>:8778']
Start Services

sudo systemctl restart prometheus
sudo systemctl start grafana-server
Subtask 2.2: Optimize Resource Allocation
Check Compute Node Utilization

openstack hypervisor stats show
Adjust Overcommit Ratios
Edit /etc/nova/nova.conf:

[DEFAULT]
cpu_allocation_ratio = 4.0
ram_allocation_ratio = 1.5
Restart Nova Services

sudo systemctl restart nova-compute
Task 3: Troubleshoot Common HCI Issues
Subtask 3.1: Diagnose Storage Issues
Check Ceph Cluster Health

sudo ceph health detail
Verify Volume Attachments

openstack volume list --status error
Subtask 3.2: Resolve Performance Bottlenecks
Check Disk I/O

sudo iostat -x 1
Adjust Ceph OSD Settings
Edit /etc/ceph/ceph.conf:

[osd]
osd_memory_target = 4294967296  # 4GB
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Deployed a hyperconverged OpenStack environment by integrating compute and Ceph storage.
Monitored performance using Prometheus and Grafana.
Troubleshot common HCI issues.
Next Steps:

Explore dynamic scaling of Ceph OSDs.
Implement high availability for hyperconverged nodes.
Troubleshooting Tips
If Ceph -s shows HEALTH_WARN, check network connectivity between nodes.
If volumes fail to create, verify cinder-volume logs:
journalctl -u cinder-volume
